      "math_academy_id": "q-66692",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "Given that $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t,\\,t{e}^{t}⟩$ what is ${r}^{′}(0)?$",
      "worked_solution": "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n- For the $x$-component $x(t)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2{sec}^{2}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(0)=2{sec}^{2}⁡0=2.\n$$\n\n- For the $y$-component $y(t)=t{e}^{t},$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}+t{e}^{t}={e}^{t}(1+t).\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)={e}^{0}(1+0)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0) & =⟨{x}^{′}(0),\\,{y}^{′}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
